Here the values are first placed on a stack. Popping removes the last original value first, so the output receives the elements in reverse order.

A quick dry run with `[4, 7, 9]` pushes `4`, `7`, `9`; popping then writes `9`, `7`, `4` into the answer vector.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> reverseArray(vector<int>& nums){
        stack<int> values;

        for (int value : nums) {
            values.push(value);
        }

        vector<int> reversed;
        reversed.reserve(nums.size());

        while (!values.empty()) {
            reversed.push_back(values.top());
            values.pop();
        }

        return reversed;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and returned vector. The input vector is left unchanged.

Kept the changes inside the given vector. The left pointer starts at the first item and the right pointer starts at the last item. Swapping them and moving both pointers inward reverses the array without needing another vector.

For `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the swaps are `1` with `4`, then `2` with `3`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> reverseArray(vector<int>& nums){
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            swap(nums[left], nums[right]);
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input vector is changed in place.

The recursive helper works on a smaller range after each swap. It exchanges the values at `left` and `right`, then calls itself for the inside part of the array.

Dry run for `[1, 2, 3]`: swap indexes `0` and `2` to get `[3, 2, 1]`, then the middle index needs no work.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] reverseArray(int[] nums) {
        reverse(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
        return nums;
    }

    private void reverse(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int temp = nums[left];
        nums[left] = nums[right];
        nums[right] = temp;

        reverse(nums, left + 1, right - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack in the worst case. The input array is modified.

Instead of swapping values, this code writes each item into its matching position in a new array. The item at index `i` belongs at index `n - 1 - i`.

This is easy to check, but it uses extra memory. For an empty input, the loop simply does not run and an empty array is returned.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] reverseArray(int[] nums) {
        int[] reversed = new int[nums.length];

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            reversed[nums.length - 1 - i] = nums[i];
        }

        return reversed;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the result array. The original array is not changed.

## Intuition
Reversing an array just means swapping the first element with the last, the second with the second-to-last, and so on until the two ends meet in the middle. That "meet in the middle" structure is naturally recursive: swap the outermost pair, then solve the same problem for the smaller inner subarray.

## Approach
Use two pointers, `left` starting at index 0 and `right` at `nums.size() - 1`.

1. **Base case:** if `left >= right`, the pointers have met (odd length) or crossed (even length), so everything is already reversed — return.
2. **Work:** swap `nums[left]` and `nums[right]`.
3. **Recursive call:** move `left` inward and `right` inward, and recurse on the remaining subarray.

The array is modified in place, so the same vector is returned at the end.

## Solution
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void function(vector<int>& nums, int left, int right) {
        // base condition
        if (left >= right) return;
        // work
        swap(nums[left], nums[right]);
        // recursive call
        function(nums, ++left, --right);
    }

    vector<int> reverseArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        function(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1);
        return nums;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** $O(n)$ — each recursive call performs one constant-time swap and shrinks the range by two, so there are about $n/2$ calls in total.

**Space Complexity:** $O(n)$ — no extra data structures are used, but the recursion goes about $n/2$ levels deep, so the call stack costs linear space. An iterative `while (left < right)` loop would bring this down to $O(1)$.53:["$

